#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "357839bc-6cf7-532d-a4cd-ce4b64aefc41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Wronskian Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f6884f33-97b6-502e-91c8-bdd3a4fc95d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21868" :question/problem "$$\nx^2y''-5xy'+\\ln(x)y=0, \\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nConsider the second-order homogeneous differential equation above. Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions, and let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian. Which differential equation must $W(x)$ satisfy?" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $x^2$ to get\n\n$$\ny''-\\frac{5}{x}y'+\\frac{\\ln(x)}{x^2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\frac{5}{x}W(x)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "bac67ae0-dc3c-528c-9e07-7a81caa86db4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21869" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/id #uuid "008cfc63-46d4-5e99-84a2-76df45a51001" :question/math-academy-id "e-21870" :question/problem "$$\ny''+4y'+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y_1$ and $y_2$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty,\\infty).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=-3.$ Use Abel's identity to conclude whether $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent on $\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -3 & =C{e}^{-8} \\\\ C & =-3{e}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSince $-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that ![](images/1e/1e26515be7d7b9ecf5cd5e1464989f6d90098247bdd95dfae75fd2545fd9bd18.png)\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are ![](images/63/6397adc2a5f64c899725f85822627697055696c0898ffb6e2ee1d2417e802caa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "12e97eec-0104-56b6-bf75-0acb8df53901" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c388af8-c41d-5c6d-8140-460cd30fd123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c388af8-c41d-5c6d-8140-460cd30fd123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365894} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a42bbe3-769a-5a38-9e0d-7251654af238" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ed9527e-50d0-53be-a3d3-45d4754a6c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-342712" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=2.$ What is $W(4)?$\n\n$W(4)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{3\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{3} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(4) & =2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3\\cdot 4}=2{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccbbbe03-535c-5a98-8643-6d9012b867e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216669} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9ab3c9-c802-5710-bfdc-a8bbd8c831cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x)/(q(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216670} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb0a8b5-e74e-55d8-9b13-0e8abbe8a521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216671} {:answer/id #uuid "70541b3e-71c2-5ccf-a23f-0f664382f814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (q(x))/(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216672}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d1285df3-060a-5fc6-be97-37ff5f0e6f8f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216668}] :question/id #uuid "72291c8c-7d08-5518-8b04-62e5d8790c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342713" :question/problem "$y^{″} + \\frac{1}{x}y^{′} + Q(x)y = 0, x > 0$\nLet $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0, ∞)$. The Wronskian satisfies $W(2) = 0$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n$-\\frac{1}{x}$ {{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbe95508-90a4-5739-800e-6730fc486775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbe95508-90a4-5739-800e-6730fc486775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316341} :answer-field/id #uuid "de9f196c-0661-5c94-9e3f-43c39b8d82e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "283b979f-8c0c-5af7-8c3b-c602adbe88b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-343272" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=2.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-5W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-5W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-5\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-5x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-5x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-5\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-5} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5\\cdot 3}=2{e}^{-10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84813773-2fbc-5752-8f31-8e547c9f79e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Wronskian Using Abel's Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95dea895-ae65-5d43-951c-870afd31fe30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95dea895-ae65-5d43-951c-870afd31fe30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315949} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a34654-f2e5-50e3-ab8b-574824d4527e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04fad153-de50-519c-b7c7-6082be26f701" :question/math-academy-id "q-343281" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+(1+x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=1.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & ={x}^{3}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{{1}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =Ce \\\\ C & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)={e}^{-1}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & ={e}^{-1}{e}^{{2}^{3}}={e}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to 